@subsection Tagged data

One way to view data abstraction is as an application of the ``principle of
least commitment.''  In implementing the complex-number system in
@ref{2.4.1}, we can use either Ben's rectangular representation or Alyssa's
polar representation.  The abstraction barrier formed by the selectors and
constructors permits us to defer to the last possible moment the choice of a
concrete representation for our data objects and thus retain maximum
flexibility in our system design.

The principle of least commitment can be carried to even further extremes.  If
we desire, we can maintain the ambiguity of representation even @emph{after} we
have designed the selectors and constructors, and elect to use both Ben's
representation @emph{and} Alyssa's representation.  If both representations are
included in a single system, however, we will need some way to distinguish data
in polar form from data in rectangular form.  Otherwise, if we were asked, for
instance, to find the @code{magnitude} of the pair (3, 4), we wouldn't know
whether to answer 5 (interpreting the number in rectangular form) or 3
(interpreting the number in polar form).  A straightforward way to accomplish
this distinction is to include a @newterm{type tag} --- the tag
@code{"rectangular"} or @code{"polar"} --- as part of each complex number.
Then when we need to manipulate a complex number we can use the tag to decide
which selector to apply.

In order to manipulate tagged data, we will assume that we have procedures that
extract from a data object the tag and the actual contents (the polar or
rectangular coordinates, in the case of a complex number).  We will also
postulate a procedure @code{attachTag} that takes a tag and contents and
produces a tagged data object.  The book builds such an object by consing the
tag onto the contents and then checking, at every extraction, that the datum
really is a pair, raising ``Bad tagged datum'' if it is not.  This edition's
tagged datum is one member of a closed union, so the tag and the contents are
the datum's two fields, and the bad-datum check is unrepresentable --- there is
no tagged datum that is not tagged:

@example typescript
export type Tagged<T extends string, A> = @{ readonly _tag: T; readonly contents: A @};

export const attachTag = <T extends string, A>(tag: T, contents: A): Tagged<T, A> => (@{
  _tag: tag,
  contents,
@});
@end example

@noindent
A complex number is the union of the two tagged shapes. The type tags of the
book become the union's discriminant:

@example typescript
export type TaggedComplex =
  | Tagged<"rectangular", ComplexPair>
  | Tagged<"polar", ComplexPair>;
@end example

@noindent
Using the discriminant, we can define the predicates @code{isRectangular} and
@code{isPolar}, which recognize rectangular and polar numbers, respectively:

@example typescript
export const isRectangular = (z: TaggedComplex): boolean => z._tag === "rectangular";

export const isPolar = (z: TaggedComplex): boolean => z._tag === "polar";
@end example

@noindent
With type tags, Ben and Alyssa can now modify their code so that their two
different representations can coexist in the same system.  Whenever Ben
constructs a complex number, he tags it as rectangular.  Whenever Alyssa
constructs a complex number, she tags it as polar.  In addition, Ben and Alyssa
must make sure that the names of their procedures do not conflict.  One way to
do this is for Ben to append the suffix @code{Rectangular} to the name of each
of his representation procedures and for Alyssa to append @code{Polar} to the
names of hers.  The suffixes are a concession to one shared namespace that
@ref{2.4.3} will remove; here they keep the two packages' procedures
distinguishable at the module's top level.  Here is Ben's revised rectangular
representation from @ref{2.4.1}:

@example typescript
export const realPartRectangular = (z: ComplexPair): number => z[0];
export const imagPartRectangular = (z: ComplexPair): number => z[1];

export const magnitudeRectangular = (z: ComplexPair): number =>
  Math.sqrt(z[0] * z[0] + z[1] * z[1]);
export const angleRectangular = (z: ComplexPair): number => Math.atan2(z[1], z[0]);

export const makeFromRealImagRectangular = (
  x: number,
  y: number,
): Tagged<"rectangular", ComplexPair> => (@{
  _tag: "rectangular",
  contents: [x, y],
@});

export const makeFromMagAngRectangular = (
  r: number,
  a: number,
): Tagged<"rectangular", ComplexPair> => (@{
  _tag: "rectangular",
  contents: [r * Math.cos(a), r * Math.sin(a)],
@});
@end example

@noindent
and here is Alyssa's revised polar representation:

@example typescript
export const realPartPolar = (z: ComplexPair): number => z[0] * Math.cos(z[1]);
export const imagPartPolar = (z: ComplexPair): number => z[0] * Math.sin(z[1]);

export const magnitudePolar = (z: ComplexPair): number => z[0];
export const anglePolar = (z: ComplexPair): number => z[1];

export const makeFromRealImagPolar = (x: number, y: number): Tagged<"polar", ComplexPair> => (@{
  _tag: "polar",
  contents: [Math.sqrt(x * x + y * y), Math.atan2(y, x)],
@});

export const makeFromMagAngPolar = (r: number, a: number): Tagged<"polar", ComplexPair> => (@{
  _tag: "polar",
  contents: [r, a],
@});
@end example

@noindent
Each generic selector is implemented as a procedure that checks the tag of its
argument and calls the appropriate procedure for handling data of that type.
For example, to obtain the real part of a complex number, @code{realPartExplicit}
examines the tag to determine whether to use Ben's @code{realPartRectangular}
or Alyssa's @code{realPartPolar}.  In either case, we read @code{contents} to
extract the bare, untagged datum and send this to the rectangular or polar
procedure as required:

@example typescript
export const realPartExplicit = (z: TaggedComplex): number =>
  z._tag === "rectangular" ? realPartRectangular(z.contents) : realPartPolar(z.contents);

export const imagPartExplicit = (z: TaggedComplex): number =>
  z._tag === "rectangular" ? imagPartRectangular(z.contents) : imagPartPolar(z.contents);

export const magnitudeExplicit = (z: TaggedComplex): number =>
  z._tag === "rectangular" ? magnitudeRectangular(z.contents) : magnitudePolar(z.contents);

export const angleExplicit = (z: TaggedComplex): number =>
  z._tag === "rectangular" ? angleRectangular(z.contents) : anglePolar(z.contents);
@end example

@noindent
The book's version of each selector ends with an else clause raising ``Unknown
type'' for an unrecognized tag.  The union has only two shapes, so the switch
of cases is exhaustive and the compiler enforces what the else clause
stipulated at run time: there is no third tag to dispatch on, and a
representation the selectors forget is a failed compile, not a failed lookup.

To implement the complex-number arithmetic operations, we can use the same
procedures @code{addComplex}, @code{subComplex}, @code{mulComplex}, and
@code{divComplex} from @ref{2.4.1}, because the selectors they call
are generic, and so will work with either representation.  The arithmetic
reads the generic selectors through one package value assembled from the
generic selectors and the two default constructors --- rectangular numbers
whenever we have real and imaginary parts, polar numbers whenever we have
magnitudes and angles:

@example typescript
export const taggedArith: ComplexArith<TaggedComplex> = @{
  realPart: realPartExplicit,
  imagPart: imagPartExplicit,
  magnitude: magnitudeExplicit,
  angle: angleExplicit,
  makeFromRealImag: makeFromRealImagRectangular,
  makeFromMagAng: makeFromMagAngPolar,
@};

export const addComplexTagged = (z1: TaggedComplex, z2: TaggedComplex): TaggedComplex =>
  addComplex(taggedArith, z1, z2);
@end example

@noindent
For example, the procedure @code{addComplexTagged} is the book's
@code{add-complex} verbatim, reading the generic selectors:

@example typescript
const u = makeFromRealImagRectangular(1, 2);
const v = makeFromRealImagRectangular(3, 4);
const sum = addComplexTagged(u, v);
realPartExplicit(sum);
// => 4
imagPartExplicit(sum);
// => 6
angleExplicit(makeFromMagAngPolar(2, Math.PI / 2));
// => 1.5707963267948966
@end example

@noindent
The resulting complex-number system has the structure shown in @ref{Figure 2.21}.
The system has been decomposed into three relatively independent parts:
the complex-number-arithmetic operations, Alyssa's polar implementation, and
Ben's rectangular implementation.  The polar and rectangular implementations
could have been written by Ben and Alyssa working separately, and both of these
can be used as underlying representations by a third programmer implementing
the complex-arithmetic procedures in terms of the abstract constructor/selector
interface.

@float
@anchor{Figure 2.21}
@ifinfo
@quotation
